#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079538} :activity/id #uuid "99731adb-b075-5d4b-865c-6112280a68cf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049131 :topic/id #uuid "dd6da6f3-04b6-5a5c-ad43-d66c3a2a88c4" :topic/math-academy-id 649} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079538 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079684 :tutorial/content "In many real situations, a quantity decreases quickly at first, but then the decrease slows down and the quantity levels off near some fixed value.\n\nFor example, consider a cup of hot coffee placed in a room. At first, the coffee cools rapidly because it is much hotter than the surrounding air. As time passes and the coffee temperature gets closer to room temperature, the cooling slows down. Eventually, the temperature levels off and approaches the room temperature rather than continuing to decrease.\n\nThis type of behavior is described by an **inhibited decay model**.\n\nAn *inhibited decay equation* models situations where the rate at which a quantity $P$ decreases depends on how far it is from a target value $a$, called the **equilibrium value**. This equilibrium value represents the level that the quantity approaches over time.\n\nAn inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}P}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -r(P-a),\n$$\n\nand an inhibited decay initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}P}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -r(P-a), \\qquad P(0) = P_0\n$$\n\nwhere $r > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $P > a$ for all $t > 0.$\n\nIn this model, the equilibrium value $a$ acts as a lower bound. The solution decreases over time but never drops below $a,$ instead approaching it more and more slowly. The figure below illustrates this behavior.\n\n![](images/1b/1b68f547db4090382b3fee626d029c619448b1c9adafe8b50312d162ff85e623.png)\n\n**Note:** Inhibited growth and inhibited decay use the same basic idea: the rate of change is proportional to the difference between $P$ and the equilibrium value $a$. The only difference is whether the solution approaches $a$ from below (growth) or from above (decay).\n\nInhibited decay models can be written in expanded form. To identify an expanded equation as inhibited decay, we factorise out the *negative* coefficient of $P.$ For example, the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = 20-5P,\n$$\n\n*is* an inhibited decay equation for $P > 4$ and $t > 0,$ since it can be expressed with $r=5$ and $a=4$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = -5(P-4).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "da7cd802-d46b-546f-bdc3-8a783a8bb999" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5513 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8b8000c0-e0de-53fb-a0bd-c942ac0f5e81" :step/math-academy-id 584874 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079539}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079539 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078582 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078374 :question/id #uuid "9d7fbff5-7db5-53e4-a17e-3fb7ac7a0069" :question/math-academy-id "e-8957"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0709c667-bc10-5bd5-a383-3fd6ca332361" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048127}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397843}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Decay Equations and Initial Value Problems"} :step/id #uuid "21c46d35-42c5-51bc-8679-d81c11066483" :step/math-academy-id 584875 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079540}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079540 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079671 :tutorial/content "Now that we’ve practiced identifying inhibited decay models, let’s look at a classic example: **Newton’s law of cooling**.\n\nThis law describes how the temperature of an object changes when it is placed in an environment with a different temperature. Formally, it states that\n\n> *The rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings.*\n\nThis leads to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}T}{\\textrm{d}t} = -k(T-T_S),\n$$\n\nwhere $T_S$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nLet’s put this into action. Suppose a thermometer is placed in a pot of boiling water and reads $100^\\circ\\textrm{C}.$ The pot is then placed in a $20^\\circ\\textrm{C}$ room, and the thermometer's reading begins to decrease.\n\nGiven that after $7$ minutes its reading is $60^\\circ\\textrm{C},$ let's model the thermometer's temperature after $t$ minutes.\n\nIn this case, the temperature of the surroundings is $T_S = 20^\\circ\\textrm{C}.$ So, by Newton's law of cooling, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}T}{\\textrm{d}t} = -k(T-20),\n$$\n\nwhere $T(t)$ is the temperature reading of the thermometer and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{T-20} & =-k\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}T}{T-20} & =-k\\int \\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}|T-20| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-20 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-20 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-20 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =20+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe'll determine the value of $K$ and $k$ in the next slide." :tutorial/id #uuid "f58653bc-cafb-532a-b92d-8183054c181b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4075 :tutorial/title "Newton's Law of Cooling"} :step/id #uuid "2253199d-d2ca-53d8-a2f6-aa2bd0cd82fd" :step/math-academy-id 584880 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079555}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079541 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078583 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078340 :question/id #uuid "8012ecda-d2f4-52de-99de-7b2ebd635661" :question/math-academy-id "e-6676"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a902da-f299-54a3-8e93-ad2716d0e887" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399580}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Decay"} :step/id #uuid "7f3084d6-abb3-5bf6-aedb-e65df0e1523b" :step/math-academy-id 584881 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079542}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079542 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078584 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078341 :question/id #uuid "e3e001a8-9294-59a4-82e6-b1122afdc72f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6677"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050905}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397884}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Decay Model"} :step/id #uuid "ae0595c0-ae02-56d3-a449-bb3345ccbe25" :step/math-academy-id 584884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079555 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079619 :tutorial/content "From the previous slide, the temperature of the thermometer is given by\n\n$$\nT = 20 + Ke^{-kt},\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ and $k$ are constants to be determined.\n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the water was $100^\\circ\\textrm{C}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0) = 100{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =20+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 100 & =20+K \\\\ K & =80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following expression for $T(t){:}$\n\n$$\nT(t) = 20 + 80e^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe can compute $k$ using the fact that $T(7)=60{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =20+80{e}^{-7k} \\\\ 40 & =80{e}^{-7k} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & ={e}^{-7k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =-7k \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}(2) & =-7k \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{ln}(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the temperature, in $^\\circ\\textrm{C},$ of the thermometer after $t$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT = 20 + 80e^{-t\\ln(2)/7}.\n$$\n\nFinally, letting [math]t_1[/math] be the time when the thermometer reads [math]40^\\circ\\textrm{C},[/math] we have the following:\n\\[\n\\begin{align}\n40 &= 20 + 80e^{-kt_1} \\\\[5pt]\n20 &= 80e^{-kt_1} \\\\[5pt]\ne^{-kt_1} &= \\dfrac14 \\\\[5pt]\n-kt_1 &= \\ln\\left(\\dfrac14\\right) \\\\[5pt]\n-kt_1 &= -2\\ln\\left(2\\right) \\\\[5pt]\nt_1 &= \\dfrac{2\\ln\\left(2\\right)}{k} \\\\[5pt]\nt_1 &= \\dfrac{2\\ln\\left(2\\right)}{\\dfrac17\\ln\\left(2\\right)} \\\\[5pt]\nt_1 &= 14\n\\end{align}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the thermometer will read [math]40^\\circ\\textrm{C}[/math] after [math]14[/math] minutes." :tutorial/id #uuid "7811364e-9b00-5478-8f01-6f3ddfee6f58" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14906 :tutorial/title "Newton's Law of Cooling (Continued)"} :step/id #uuid "7feac704-66a6-58a5-be02-6d1d595f029b" :step/math-academy-id 608082 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079541}}] :activity/title "Inhibited Decay Models With First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078671}]}
